{"qid":"Q128904","names":["Elam"],"title":"Elam","endonym":null,"capsule":"Elam was one of the earliest complex polities in the ancient Near East, centered on Susa in southwestern Iran (modern Khuzestan) and the highland city of Anshan, with a continuous political presence attested from roughly the Proto-Elamite period (c. 3200 BC) through four major historical phases ending around 539 BC. The Elamite language, a probable isolate with no established relatives, was used in royal inscriptions and remained in administrative use even under the Achaemenids. The kingdom's greatest military reach came under the Shutrukid dynasty (c. 1210–1100 BC) when Elam raided Babylonia and carried off the stele of Hammurabi and other monuments to Susa. Ashurbanipal's campaigns of 647–646 BC broke Elamite power irrecoverably, sacking Susa and destroying its temples, and the remnant was incorporated into the Achaemenid Empire after Cyrus II's conquests.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core territory around Susa and Anshan is documented through extensive cuneiform and Elamite-language inscriptions; outer tribal zones and the full extent of Proto-Elamite territory (extending the dataset's from-year to c. 3200 BC) are reconstructed from archaeological survey and remain conjectural, especially for the earlier millennia.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Elam","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Elam"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Elam","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elam"}],"britannica":"place/Elam","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Elam","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Peli (Awan I)","nativeName":"𒁉𒇷","roman":"Peli